              "output": "1  import { request } from './http';\n2  \n3  export async function uploadChunk(chunk: Buffer, attempt = 0) {\n4    try {\n5      return await request('PUT', chunk);\n6    } catch (err) {\n7      await sleep(delay(attempt));\n8      return uploadChunk(chunk, attempt + 1);\n9    }\n10 }"
            }
          },
          {
            "uuid": "a-1",
            "role": "assistant",
            "text": "## Why the uploads stall\n\nThe retry loop in `uploadChunk` waits for the *previous* delay to finish **before** it computes the next one, so a burst of failures compounds instead of spreading out. Two things make it worse:\n\n- the delay doubles on every attempt but is never capped\n- the jitter is added to the delay instead of replacing part of it\n\n### What I would change\n\n1. Cap the delay at 30 seconds\n2. Apply *full jitter*, a random value between zero and the capped delay\n3. Stop after `maxAttempts` and surface the last error\n\nHere is the shape of the fix:\n\n```ts\nexport function nextDelay(attempt: number, base = 500, cap = 30_000): number {\n  const ceiling = Math.min(cap, base * 2 ** attempt);\n  return Math.floor(Math.random() * ceiling);\n}\n```\n\nAnd how to check it from the command line:\n\n```bash\nnpm test -- --grep \"nextDelay\"\nnode scripts/simulate-retries.mjs --attempts 8 --seed 7\n```\n\n| Attempt | Old delay | New ceiling |\n|---------|-----------|-------------|\n| 1       | 500 ms    | 500 ms      |\n| 4       | 4 s       | 4 s         |\n| 8       | 64 s      | 30 s        |\n\n> Backoff without a cap is a polite way of waiting forever.\n\nThe reasoning follows the [exponential backoff guide](https://example.com/docs/retries), which recommends full jitter for exactly this case."
          },
          {
            "uuid": "u-2",
            "role": "user",
            "text": "Good catch. Fix the backoff, but keep the public API unchanged."
          },
